from common import Batch
b=Batch('JS',1)
def q(*args,**kw): b.add(*args,**kw)
q('P','Declarations','const object mutation','What does this configuration update log?','dark','const protects the binding, not object properties.~The existing object is mutated and its new theme is read. Reassigning config itself would throw.','Separate the variable from its value.','Which operation changes the binding?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/const','const config = { theme: "light" };\nconfig.theme = "dark";\nconsole.log(config.theme);')
q('M','Types','Primitive identity','Which statement explains why two separately created objects with equal fields are not strictly equal?','Object equality compares identity, not field contents.','Strict equality does not recursively compare objects. Equal primitive strings compare by value; two object literals create distinct identities.','Think about references.','Does === inspect properties?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Strict_equality',wrong=['Strict equality always converts objects to strings.','Objects cannot be compared with ===.','Equal objects compare true only when frozen.'])
q('P','Coercion','String addition','What does this checkout calculation log?','105\n5','The first + has a string operand and concatenates.~Subtraction converts the string to a number. An invalid numeric string would produce NaN.','The operator matters.','Compare + with subtraction.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Addition','const price = "10";\nconsole.log(price + 5);\nconsole.log(price - 5);')
q('B','Defaults','Falsy fallback','A valid quantity of zero becomes one. Identify the bug and correct the expression.','|| replaces every falsy value. Use: const quantity = input ?? 1;','Zero is falsy, so || chooses 1.~?? substitutes only for null or undefined; it preserves zero, false and empty strings.','Is zero missing?','Compare falsy with nullish.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Nullish_coalescing','const input = 0;\nconst quantity = input || 1;')
q('P','Declarations','var hoisting','What does this function log?','undefined\nready','The var binding exists at function entry, initialized to undefined.~Assignment happens later. This behavior differs from an uninitialized let binding.','Separate declaration and assignment.','What initial value does var receive?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/var','function initialize() {\n  console.log(status);\n  var status = "ready";\n  console.log(status);\n}\ninitialize();')
q('P','Declarations','Temporal dead zone','What error name is logged?','ReferenceError','The block creates its own status binding before execution.~Reading that binding before its let initialization throws; the outer variable is shadowed.','Look at the whole block.','Does the inner declaration shadow immediately?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/let','let status = "outer";\ntry {\n  console.log(status);\n  let status = "inner";\n} catch (error) {\n  console.log(error.name);\n}')
q('C','Scope','Lexical lookup','How does a function choose which variable named user to read?','It searches its lexical scope, then enclosing scopes. The scopes come from where the function was defined. Calling it elsewhere does not change that chain.','Dynamic caller scope is not used for ordinary identifier lookup. A nearer declaration shadows an outer declaration, including during its temporal dead zone.','Consider the definition site.','Does moving the call move the declaration?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Guide/Grammar_and_types#variable_scope')
q('P','Arrays','Nonmutating map','What does this price transformation log?','2,4\n1,2','map calls the callback for each present entry and builds a new array.~The callback only multiplies numbers, so the source is unchanged. Mutating object entries inside map would be different.','Track both arrays.','Does multiplication assign to the source?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/map','const prices = [1, 2];\nconst doubled = prices.map(price => price * 2);\nconsole.log(doubled.join(","));\nconsole.log(prices.join(","));')
q('M','Arrays','filter selection','Which expression retains only active records without changing the source array?','records.filter(record => record.active)','filter selects entries using a truthy callback result. map transforms each entry, and find returns only the first match. Retained objects are shared references.','You need a subset.','Which method returns all matches?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/filter',wrong=['records.map(record => record.active)','records.find(record => record.active)','records.forEach(record => record.active)'])
q('P','Arrays','find absence','What is logged when no matching product exists?','undefined','find tests entries in order and returns the first match.~No predicate result is true, so it returns undefined rather than an empty array.','Distinguish one match from many.','What is the not-found sentinel?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/find','const products = [{ id: 1 }];\nconsole.log(products.find(product => product.id === 9));')
q('P','Functions','Default parameters','What does this greet function log?','Guest\nnull','Omitting the argument supplies undefined and activates the default.~Explicit null does not activate a parameter default.','Which missing value triggers a default?','Compare omitted and null arguments.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/Default_parameters','function greet(name = "Guest") {\n  console.log(String(name));\n}\ngreet();\ngreet(null);')
q('P','Objects','Destructuring fallback','What does this options destructuring log?','0 3','The property count exists with value zero, so its default is unused.~The absent retries property yields undefined and takes its default. Null would also be preserved.','Defaults test undefined.','Is zero undefined?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Destructuring','const { count = 5, retries = 3 } = { count: 0 };\nconsole.log(count, retries);')
q('B','Arrays','Numeric sorting','Why is the numeric ranking wrong, and which line fixes it?','sort() compares string representations by default. Use: scores.sort((a, b) => a - b);','The default sort compares "10" before "2".~A numeric comparator returns a negative, zero or positive difference. sort still mutates scores.','Inspect the default comparator.','Provide an ordering based on numbers.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/sort','const scores = [2, 10, 1];\nscores.sort();')
q('M','Numbers','NaN validation','Which check rejects NaN without coercing a numeric string?','Number.isNaN(value)','Number.isNaN checks whether the value is the numeric NaN. Global isNaN coerces first, and NaN is not equal to itself.','Avoid implicit conversion.','Compare the global and Number methods.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number/isNaN',wrong=['value === NaN','isNaN(value)','value == NaN'])
q('P','Objects','Optional chaining','What does this account lookup log?','anonymous','profile is null, so optional chaining stops and yields undefined.~The nullish fallback then supplies anonymous. Optional chaining does not validate a present non-callable method.','Find the short-circuit point.','What value does the stopped chain produce?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Optional_chaining','const account = { profile: null };\nconsole.log(account.profile?.name ?? "anonymous");')
q('P','Arrays','slice versus splice','What is logged after removing the middle item?','b\na,c','splice removes one entry starting at index 1 and returns the removed entries.~The original array now contains a and c; slice would not remove them.','Identify the mutating method.','Track the return value separately.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/splice','const items = ["a", "b", "c"];\nconst removed = items.splice(1, 1);\nconsole.log(removed.join(","));\nconsole.log(items.join(","));')
q('C','Types','undefined and null','How would you distinguish absent optional input from an explicitly cleared value?','Use an explicit contract for undefined and null. For example, undefined can mean leave unchanged and null can mean clear. Check them separately when those operations differ.','JavaScript does not assign business meaning automatically. A nullish fallback merges these cases and would lose the distinction.','Define the API contract.','Should clearing equal omission?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/null')
q('P','Arrays','Rest parameters','What does the sum call log?','6','The rest parameter collects three arguments into a real array.~reduce starts at zero and adds 1, 2 and 3. The initial value also handles no arguments.','Rest collects trailing arguments.','Follow the accumulator.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/rest_parameters','function sum(...values) {\n  return values.reduce((total, value) => total + value, 0);\n}\nconsole.log(sum(1, 2, 3));')
q('P','Objects','Spread precedence','Which theme is logged after merging settings?','dark','Object spread copies enumerable own properties in order.~The later explicit theme overwrites the earlier theme. Nested objects would remain shared.','Read assignments left to right.','Which value is assigned last?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Spread_syntax','const defaults = { theme: "light", size: 12 };\nconst settings = { ...defaults, theme: "dark" };\nconsole.log(settings.theme);')
q('M','Equality','Strict comparison','An API returns the string "7". Which comparison confirms that it is a string with that exact value?','value === "7"','Strict equality checks type and value for primitives. Loose equality with 7 also accepts the number 7 and therefore does not establish the required type.','The type is part of the requirement.','Avoid coercive equality.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Equality',wrong=['value == 7','Number(value) === 7','value >= 7'])
q('P','Collections','Set uniqueness','What does deduplicating these tags log?','js,react','Set keeps each distinct primitive value once, in insertion order.~Spreading creates an array of those values. Distinct objects would not deduplicate by fields.','Track first insertion.','Does the repeated string add an entry?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Set','const tags = ["js", "react", "js"];\nconsole.log([...new Set(tags)].join(","));')
q('C','Collections','Map versus object','Why use a Map when keys are DOM nodes?','Map supports object keys by identity. It has explicit get, set and has operations. Use WeakMap instead when the mapping should not keep those nodes alive.','Ordinary object property keys are strings or symbols, so object keys are coerced. Map does not stringify its keys.','Think about key types.','Would a node become a property-name string?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map')
q('P','Control flow','Short-circuit evaluation','How many times is load called? Read the logged count.','0','The false left operand stops && before load runs.~The true left operand stops || before load runs. These operators return operands rather than always returning booleans.','Some operands are skipped.','Evaluate each left operand first.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Logical_AND','let calls = 0;\nconst load = () => ++calls;\nfalse && load();\ntrue || load();\nconsole.log(calls);')
q('B','Arrays','forEach return value','This transformation returns undefined. Identify the bug and fix the line.','forEach does not collect callback results. Use: const names = users.map(user => user.name);','The callback return is ignored by forEach.~map creates the desired array. Neither method deep-clones returned objects.','Check the method return contract.','Which method collects callback results?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/forEach','const users = [{ name: "Ada" }];\nconst names = users.forEach(user => user.name);')
q('P','Strings','Immutability','What does this normalized search term log?','  React  \nreact','trim and toLowerCase return new strings.~The original binding retains its original whitespace and case. Assignment would be needed to replace it.','String methods return values.','Is term reassigned?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/trim','const term = "  React  ";\nconst normalized = term.trim().toLowerCase();\nconsole.log(term);\nconsole.log(normalized);')
q('M','Arrays','Array detection','Which check reliably distinguishes arrays from plain objects?','Array.isArray(value)','Array.isArray checks the array brand, including arrays from other realms. typeof reports object for both arrays and ordinary objects.','typeof is broad.','Look for an array-specific check.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/isArray',wrong=['typeof value === "array"','typeof value === "object"','value.constructor.name === "Array"'])
q('C','Functions','Declaration versus expression','Why can a function declaration be called earlier in its scope, while a const-bound function expression cannot?','A function declaration is initialized during scope setup. A const binding remains uninitialized until its declaration executes. Calling the latter early reads it in the temporal dead zone.','This is not evidence that source lines physically move. Function expressions are values created when their expressions execute.','Compare binding initialization times.','Which binding exists but is uninitialized?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/function')
q('P','Control flow','Block scope','What is logged after this conditional block?','outside','The inner let creates a distinct block-scoped binding.~Leaving the block exposes the unchanged outer binding again. A plain assignment without let would modify it.','Count the declarations.','Are these the same binding?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Guide/Grammar_and_types#variable_scope','let label = "outside";\nif (true) {\n  let label = "inside";\n}\nconsole.log(label);')
q('W','Arrays','Sum with empty input','Implement total(prices) for an array of finite numbers, including an empty array.','function total(prices) {\n  return prices.reduce((sum, price) => sum + price, 0);\n}\n// Time: O(n). Auxiliary space: O(1).','Start the accumulator at zero.~Add each price once.~An empty array returns zero; omitting the initial value would throw for empty input.','Choose an identity value.','What is the additive identity?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/reduce','// Implement total(prices).\n// Inputs contain only finite numbers.')
q('P','Objects','Missing property versus own undefined','What do these ownership checks log?','true\nfalse','a has an own property named value even though its value is undefined.~b lacks that own property. Comparing values alone would not distinguish them.','Presence differs from value.','Use own-property semantics.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/hasOwn','const a = { value: undefined };\nconst b = {};\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(a, "value"));\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(b, "value"));')
q('M','Errors','try catch scope','Which error can this surrounding try/catch catch directly?','A synchronous throw while executing work().','try/catch catches throws during its dynamic execution. A later timer callback runs after that stack has ended, and an unawaited rejected promise is not a synchronous throw.','Consider when work fails.','Is the failure on this call stack?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/try...catch','try {\n  work();\n} catch (error) {\n  report(error);\n}',wrong=['Every rejection from promises created by work().','Every error in future timers created by work().','Only SyntaxError, never Error.'])
q('S','Numbers','Decimal precision','A cart total is 0.30000000000000004. How should a UI handle prices with a known two-decimal currency scale?','Represent amounts in integer minor units where feasible. Sum those integers and format the result for display. Define rounding explicitly at conversion boundaries.','Binary floating point cannot represent every decimal exactly. Formatting hides display noise but does not itself make repeated arithmetic exact.','Separate representation from display.','Can the domain use cents?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number/EPSILON')
q('P','Strings','Template interpolation','What text is logged?','Hello, Ada!','The template literal evaluates the name expression.~Its string value is inserted between the literal parts. Interpolation does not sanitize text for HTML insertion.','Locate the substitution.','What does name hold?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Template_literals','const name = "Ada";\nconsole.log(`Hello, ${name}!`);')
q('P','Arrays','some and every','What do these inventory checks log?','true\nfalse','some succeeds because one stock value is positive.~every fails because zero is not positive. On an empty array, some is false and every is true.','The methods express different quantifiers.','Is one match enough?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/every','const stock = [0, 2];\nconsole.log(stock.some(n => n > 0));\nconsole.log(stock.every(n => n > 0));')
q('M','Numbers','Integer parsing','For a quantity field, which expression converts "12" to a number but rejects the whole string "12px" as NaN?','Number(value)','Number requires the entire string to represent a number. parseInt and parseFloat accept numeric prefixes. Empty or whitespace-only strings need separate validation because Number converts them to zero.','Prefix parsing may be too permissive.','Must the whole string be numeric?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number/Number',wrong=['parseInt(value, 10)','parseFloat(value)','value.slice(0, 2) * 1'])
q('C','Objects','Pass by value','Does passing an object to a function let the function replace the caller’s variable?','No, the reference value is passed by value. Mutating the referenced object is visible to both sides. Reassigning the parameter only replaces the local binding.','Shared mutation is often mistaken for pass-by-reference. The caller’s binding is not an alias of the callee’s parameter binding.','Distinguish a reference value from a variable.','Mutation and reassignment differ.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Guide/Functions#passing_arguments')
q('U','Defaults','Defaults at API boundaries','Follow-up to JS-004: when should an API preserve an empty string rather than replace it with a default?','Preserve it when empty text is a valid explicit value. Use a nullish check when only missing values should trigger defaults. Validate blank text separately if the domain forbids it.','|| combines absence with every falsy value. Defaulting and validation are distinct responsibilities and should follow the input contract.','Ask what empty text means.','Can clearing a field be intentional?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Nullish_coalescing')
q('M','Arrays','includes membership','Which method finds NaN in an array using SameValueZero comparison?','values.includes(NaN)','includes treats NaN as matching NaN. indexOf uses strict equality and does not find it. Converting the array to a string changes the problem.','Compare equality algorithms.','Which membership API handles NaN?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/includes',wrong=['values.indexOf(NaN) !== -1','values.some(value => value === NaN)','values.findIndex(value => value === NaN) !== -1'])
q('T','Closures','Independent factory state','Trace the counters and give the exact output.','1 2 1','Each factory call creates a new n binding.~a retains the first binding and increments it twice.~c retains a separate binding. Closures share state only when they capture the same environment.','Count factory calls.','Which functions share an environment?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Closures','function counter() {\n  let n = 0;\n  return () => ++n;\n}\nconst a = counter();\nconst c = counter();\nconsole.log(a(), a(), c());')
q('T','Closures','Loop bindings','What does the callback collection log?','0,1,2','The for loop creates a fresh let binding for each iteration.~Each callback closes over its iteration binding.~Calling them later reads 0, 1 and 2. Using var here would share one final binding.','Focus on per-iteration bindings.','Do the closures capture one i or several?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Closures#creating_closures_in_loops_a_common_mistake','const readers = [];\nfor (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n  readers.push(() => i);\n}\nconsole.log(readers.map(read => read()).join(","));')
q('P','this','Explicit receiver','What does this method borrowing log?','Grace','call invokes label with an explicit this value.~The property lookup reads other.name. The original owning object does not permanently bind the method.','Functions can be borrowed.','Which object is passed to call?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Function/call','const person = {\n  name: "Ada",\n  label() { return this.name; }\n};\nconst other = { name: "Grace" };\nconsole.log(person.label.call(other));')
q('B','this','Detached method','Why does the detached call fail in strict mode, and how can you preserve the receiver?','The call has no receiver, so this is undefined. Use: const read = cart.total.bind(cart);','Reading cart.total obtains the function without binding it.~Calling read() loses cart as this.~bind creates a function with cart as its receiver. An arrow wrapper would also preserve the method call.','Inspect the call expression.','Is there an object before the dot at invocation?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Function/bind','"use strict";\nconst cart = {\n  amount: 20,\n  total() { return this.amount; }\n};\nconst read = cart.total;\nread();')
q('P','this','Lexical arrow receiver','What is logged by this returned arrow?','Ada','make is invoked as a method of user.~The arrow captures that invocation’s this.~call cannot replace an arrow’s lexical this.','Find where the arrow is created.','Can call rebind an arrow?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/Arrow_functions','const user = {\n  name: "Ada",\n  make() { return () => this.name; }\n};\nconst read = user.make();\nconsole.log(read.call({ name: "Lin" }));')
q('C','this','Construction binding','How does new affect this for an ordinary constructible function?','It creates an object linked to the constructor’s prototype. It calls the constructor with that object as this. An explicitly returned object can replace the newly created result.','A returned primitive normally does not replace the instance. Arrow functions are not constructors and cannot be invoked with new.','Think about instance creation.','What if the constructor returns an object?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/new')
q('P','Prototypes','Inherited lookup','What does this object log before and after shadowing?','guest\nadmin guest','The first read follows the prototype to base.role.~Assignment creates user’s own role property.~base remains unchanged; assignment does not normally edit the prototype’s data property.','Separate own and inherited properties.','Where does the assignment land?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Inheritance_and_the_prototype_chain','const base = { role: "guest" };\nconst user = Object.create(base);\nconsole.log(user.role);\nuser.role = "admin";\nconsole.log(user.role, base.role);')
q('M','Prototypes','Ownership checks','Which expression checks only an own property even if obj has a misleading hasOwnProperty field?','Object.hasOwn(obj, key)','Object.hasOwn is independent of methods stored on obj. The in operator includes inherited properties, and calling a shadowed method can fail.','Avoid trusting instance methods.','Which API accepts the object explicitly?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object/hasOwn',wrong=['key in obj','obj.hasOwnProperty(key)','obj[key] !== undefined'])
q('P','Objects','Getter evaluation','What does this lazy-looking property read log?','4 4 2','Each access invokes the getter anew.~The getter increments reads and returns 4.~Getters do not automatically memoize, and their side effects make property reads observable.','A getter is executable code.','How many reads occur before reads is logged?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/get','let reads = 0;\nconst product = {\n  get price() {\n    reads++;\n    return 4;\n  }\n};\nconsole.log(product.price, product.price, reads);')
q('P','Objects','Setter normalization','What does assigning this name log?','Ada','Assignment invokes the setter with the provided text.~The setter stores the trimmed value in a different property.~Using this.name inside the setter would recursively invoke itself.','Follow the assignment hook.','Which property stores the backing value?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Functions/set','const user = {\n  _name: "",\n  set name(value) { this._name = value.trim(); }\n};\nuser.name = " Ada ";\nconsole.log(user._name);')
q('B','Immutability','Shallow copy alias','Why did changing copy update original, and which replacement line makes this specific update independent?','The nested profile is shared. Use: const copy = { ...original, profile: { ...original.profile } };','Spread copies the outer object only.~Both profiles initially reference one object.~Copying profile creates a separate path to the edited field; deeper edited paths would also need copying.','Inspect nested references.','How many objects does the spread create?','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Spread_syntax#copying_and_merging_objects','const original = { profile: { city: "Pune" } };\nconst copy = { ...original };\ncopy.profile.city = "Delhi";')
q('W','Immutability','Array replacement','Write rename(items, id, name) that replaces matching records without mutating input. Each record has a unique id.','function rename(items, id, name) {\n  return items.map(item =>\n    item.id === id ? { ...item, name } : item\n  );\n}\n// Time: O(n). Additional array space: O(n).','Visit each entry once.~Create a new object only for the matching ID.~Unchanged entries retain identity; no match still returns a new array. Nested fields remain shared.','Preserve unchanged references.','Combine a transform with an ID check.','mdn:Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/map','// Implement rename(items, id, name).\n// Do not mutate items or its records.')
